      "expect_output": "x = 5\n"
     }
    }
   },
   "outputs": []
  },
  {
   "id": "casm-readme-md-3",
   "kind": {
    "type": "markdown"
   },
   "source": "```bash\ncrushc add.crush --emit casm\n```\n\n```casm\n.func main\n    PUSH 3\n    PUSH 2\n    CALL add\n    STORE 0\n    PUSH_STR \"x = \"\n    LOAD 0\n    CAP_CALL \"io.print\" 2\n    PUSH_NULL\n    RET\n.func add\n    STORE 0\n    STORE 1\n    LOAD 0\n    LOAD 1\n    ADD\n    RET\n```\n\nTwo things to notice, both explained in [Program Structure](https://docs.crushlang.org/casm/structure.html): arguments\nare pushed in **reverse** order (`3` then `2`), so the callee's first `STORE` pops the\nfirst argument into slot 0, the second `STORE` the next one, and so on; and all top-level statements live in a\nfunction called `main`.\n\n## Next steps\n\n- **[Program Structure](https://docs.crushlang.org/casm/structure.html)** — the JSON IR, the text assembly and the CVM1 binary layout.\n- **[Instruction Reference](https://docs.crushlang.org/casm/instructions.html)** — every opcode, with stack effects.\n- **[Examples](https://docs.crushlang.org/casm/examples.html)** — hand-written programs, all run by the guide's checker.\n- **[Serialization](https://docs.crushlang.org/casm/serialization.html)** — `.casm`, `.casmb`, `.cvm1` and the version gates.\n- **[Crush Language Guide](https://docs.crushlang.org/crush/index.html)** — the source language that compiles to it.\n",
